[2017-10-29T14:05:16.388+0000] {quote}
I suggest we introduce an extra REGISTERING state, which we enter after a (re)registration request is sent. This way we can drop all registered / reregistered confirmations coming from the previous master during the DISCONNECTED stage.
{quote}
So you are suggesting that we can set slave's state to {{REGISTERING}} after {{RegisterSlaveMessage}} / {{ReregisterSlaveMessage}} is sent in {{Slave::doReliableRegistration()}}, and in {{Slave::registered()}} / {{Slave::reregistered()}}, we should ignore any registered / reregistered confirmations if the slave is in {{DISCONNECTED}} state but only handle them in {{REGISTERING}} state, right?

I agree it can fix the issue described in this ticket since the second {{SlaveRegisteredMessage}} arrives onto agent before {{ReregisterSlaveMessage}} is sent, i.e., the agent is still in {{DISCONNECTED}} state, so the second {{SlaveRegisteredMessage}} will be ignored. But what if the second {{SlaveRegisteredMessage}} arrives onto agent *after* {{ReregisterSlaveMessage}} is sent? As per your suggestion, we will set agent's state to {{REGISTERING}} when {{ReregisterSlaveMessage}} is sent, so when the second {{SlaveRegisteredMessage}} arrives, we will not ignore it and will set agent's state to {{RUNNING}}, and then when {{SlaveReregisteredMessage}} arrives, it will be ignored which seems incorrect.

And I think in a production environment, there must be multiple masters (e.g., 3 or 5) for HA purpose, in such environment, if {{Slave::detected()}} is invoked, the elected master must be different from the previous one, so when the second {{SlaveRegisteredMessage}} arrives onto agent, it will be ignored in {{Slave::registered()}} because it is not from the expected master (the expected master is already set to a different master in {{Slave::detected()}}). So it seems this issue will only happen when we try to simulate new master detected event with a single master in our test, but not likely happen in the real production environment.

description:
The issue is easily reproducible if one launches a task group and the taks nested container defines the same set of environment variables as the executor. In those circumstances, the following [snippet is activated|https://github.com/apache/mesos/blob/285d82080748cd69044c226950274c7046048c4b/src/slave/containerizer/mesos/launch.cpp#L1057]:

{code}
      if (environment.contains(name) && environment[name] != value) {
        cout << "Overwriting environment variable '" << name << "'" << endl;
      }
{code}

But this is not the only time that this file writes into {{cout}}.

This may be a bad idea because applications which consume the standard output of a task may end up being corrupted by the container manager output. In these cases, writing to {{cerr}} should be the right approach.

last_two_comments:
This is not an issue as the master already makes sure to not apply updates to unknown operations. The master can only know the operation after successful reconciliation (via {{UpdateSlaveMessage}}).

{code:language=c++}
// From Master::updateOperationStatus.
Operation* operation = slave->getOperation(uuid.get());
if (operation == nullptr) {
  LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to find the operation '"
             << update.status().operation_id() << "' (uuid: "
             << uuid->toString() << ") for "
             << (frameworkId.isSome()
                   ? "framework " + stringify(frameworkId.get())
                   : "an operator API call")
             << " on agent " << slaveId;
  return;
}
{code}

